WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.752 --> 00:00:08.588
Thank you all so much, how's it going?

00:00:08.588 --> 00:00:11.424
How are you doing, TED, TEDWomen?

00:00:11.925 --> 00:00:14.594
They brought me in
for a bit of comic relief.

00:00:15.303 --> 00:00:18.139
I’m so excited to be here at TEDWomen.

00:00:18.181 --> 00:00:20.725
You know, I think I speak for myself,

00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:22.894
but also for all of us,

00:00:22.894 --> 00:00:25.730
when I think I'm really inspired,
you know, and personally,

00:00:25.772 --> 00:00:29.859
I feel like I'm not going to stop
until every person,

00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:31.903
living or dead, is a woman.

00:00:31.945 --> 00:00:34.072
(Laughter)

00:00:34.114 --> 00:00:35.323
Yeah.

00:00:36.074 --> 00:00:38.660
Yeah, I think that's where we're at.

00:00:38.702 --> 00:00:40.203
(Laughter)

00:00:41.246 --> 00:00:44.958
No, but I do hate when people say
that God is a woman, right?

00:00:44.958 --> 00:00:46.167
I hate that.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.753
I really, I think she would have
done better, you know?

00:00:48.753 --> 00:00:49.921
No.

00:00:49.963 --> 00:00:51.631
(Laughter and applause)

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:54.593
It's like, if God is a woman,
she's a real guy's gal,

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:56.136
if you know what I mean?

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.598
(Laughter)

00:01:00.348 --> 00:01:02.517
I'm going to do a couple
songs for you guys.

00:01:03.268 --> 00:01:08.690
So this first one I wrote before lockdown,
but it became especially relevant.

00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:13.361
And it's about this thing I do
where I like somebody, I have a crush,

00:01:13.403 --> 00:01:15.488
or maybe we go on a couple of dates.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:19.034
And instead of letting things
unfold naturally,

00:01:19.075 --> 00:01:23.121
I'll sort of accidentally scroll
their Instagram back until 2012.

00:01:23.121 --> 00:01:24.414
(Laughter)

00:01:24.831 --> 00:01:26.750
This song is about that.

00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:28.335
(Music)

00:01:28.376 --> 00:01:34.382
(Singing) I learned too much about you
too soon because I couldn’t wait.

00:01:34.924 --> 00:01:38.720
Yes, I internet-stalked you

00:01:38.762 --> 00:01:43.600
to the point that we can no longer date.

00:01:44.476 --> 00:01:46.978
Because I know your mother's name

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.980
and your father's name

00:01:49.022 --> 00:01:51.024
and your sister's name

00:01:51.024 --> 00:01:53.943
and your cousin's husband's name.

00:01:54.402 --> 00:01:59.616
I know that your uncle
recently passed away,

00:01:59.658 --> 00:02:01.868
and I'm so sorry about that, babe.

00:02:02.202 --> 00:02:04.829
I know every girl that you've ever dated,

00:02:04.871 --> 00:02:09.000
and the exact day
you stopped being happy --

00:02:09.042 --> 00:02:10.210
Your posture changed.

00:02:10.585 --> 00:02:14.381
I know that you and your friends hike.

00:02:14.422 --> 00:02:15.548
A lot.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.009
It's too much, we'll change that.

00:02:18.551 --> 00:02:24.891
Seen every photo tagged of you
on the entire internet.

00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:28.978
I know you dropped out of college,
but your Spanish, it's polished,

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.774
and you own approximately five shirts.

00:02:32.816 --> 00:02:36.695
The police are outside my door,

00:02:36.695 --> 00:02:42.701
but I'm clutching your hair doll
I made on the floor.

00:02:43.743 --> 00:02:48.957
If they ever tell people how often
other people look at their profiles,

00:02:48.957 --> 00:02:50.625
I will kill myself.

00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:52.627
(Laughter)

00:02:54.003 --> 00:02:57.757
So goodbye,

00:02:57.757 --> 00:03:01.469
lover I stalked.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.972
(Applause)

00:03:04.013 --> 00:03:05.223
Thank you.

00:03:05.223 --> 00:03:06.641
(Applause)

00:03:06.683 --> 00:03:08.059
It's vulnerable.

00:03:08.059 --> 00:03:09.811
I feel like it's a thing people say

00:03:09.853 --> 00:03:12.731
when conversation gets, kind of,
boring at parties, you know,

00:03:12.731 --> 00:03:14.733
they're like, "Did you hear there's an app

00:03:14.774 --> 00:03:17.026
where you can see
who looks at your Instagram?"

00:03:17.026 --> 00:03:18.403
It's like, that's not funny.

00:03:18.403 --> 00:03:20.572
It's not funny,
it's not a cool thing to say.

00:03:21.406 --> 00:03:24.951
I feel like there are people here
who have been married for 40 years, like,

00:03:24.951 --> 00:03:26.745
"What the hell is she talking about?"

00:03:26.745 --> 00:03:28.037
Yeah, it's complicated now.

00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:29.372
It's gotten complicated in.

00:03:29.414 --> 00:03:32.292
(Laughter)

00:03:32.625 --> 00:03:34.794
Audience: We’re not so ancient!

00:03:34.836 --> 00:03:36.004
MB: No, I know.

00:03:36.963 --> 00:03:39.257
You don't have to be old
to be married 40 years,

00:03:39.257 --> 00:03:41.468
some people get married
when they're, like, 20.

00:03:41.509 --> 00:03:42.635
Not me.

00:03:42.677 --> 00:03:43.803
(Laughter)

00:03:44.888 --> 00:03:48.600
I do understand why, like,
baby boomers are sort of afraid

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.935
now that millennials
are getting older, you know,

00:03:50.935 --> 00:03:53.188
now that we're getting
real jobs, I do get that.

00:03:53.229 --> 00:03:56.608
You know, it's hard to imagine
a millennial in a serious job, right?

00:03:56.649 --> 00:03:58.985
Can you imagine a millennial as a doctor?

00:03:58.985 --> 00:04:00.153
You know?

00:04:00.445 --> 00:04:03.364
Like, "Not to be a bitch,
but you have cancer."

00:04:03.406 --> 00:04:07.869
(Laughter)

00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:12.665
You know, like hands your prognosis like,

00:04:12.707 --> 00:04:13.958
"I'm really sorry.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.335
Actually, I'm not sorry."

00:04:15.376 --> 00:04:18.922
(Laughter)

00:04:19.756 --> 00:04:21.549
I'll tell you a little about myself.

00:04:21.925 --> 00:04:24.677
I had a pretty crazy last couple of years.

00:04:24.677 --> 00:04:28.640
So basically, I had this set come out
for Comedy Central, a short set,

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.767
that came out April 2020.

00:04:30.809 --> 00:04:33.228
So right when we had just
gotten into lockdown.

00:04:33.228 --> 00:04:35.438
And then cut to like six months later,

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.818
one of the songs I did from
that set went super viral on TikTok.

00:04:40.151 --> 00:04:42.403
And I was not on TikTok, OK,

00:04:42.445 --> 00:04:46.074
because I'm 32, so I, sort of,
didn't know we were allowed.

00:04:46.115 --> 00:04:47.575
(Laughter)

00:04:47.575 --> 00:04:49.160
But we are.

00:04:49.202 --> 00:04:53.289
And it was crazy because there's all
these people lip-synching my song,

00:04:53.331 --> 00:04:54.791
but in my real life,

00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:58.670
I had just started this job
working customer support

00:04:58.711 --> 00:04:59.921
for a food delivery app.

00:04:59.963 --> 00:05:02.590
So answering phone calls
for customer support.

00:05:02.632 --> 00:05:05.802
The worst job I've ever had in my life.

00:05:05.802 --> 00:05:08.137
So it was this really weird juxtaposition

00:05:08.179 --> 00:05:12.016
because I'm having this huge career moment
when I look at my phone, right?

00:05:12.016 --> 00:05:15.228
People are tweeting at me like,
"We love you. You're a genius."

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.564
And then for eight hours a day,
people are, like,

00:05:17.605 --> 00:05:19.274
"You're a bitch, where's my food?"

00:05:19.315 --> 00:05:21.442
(Laughter)

00:05:22.193 --> 00:05:24.821
And it was really interesting
to experience.

00:05:25.405 --> 00:05:27.490
So I'm going to play
this song for you guys,

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.493
and then I'm going to go over
some of my favorite male reactions

00:05:30.535 --> 00:05:33.621
that I got on the internet afterwards.

00:05:33.663 --> 00:05:36.916
So this song, maybe you've heard it,
maybe you haven't.

00:05:36.958 --> 00:05:39.210
It is the true story

00:05:39.252 --> 00:05:42.714
about the first American woman
who they ever sent to space,

00:05:42.755 --> 00:05:44.966
whose name was Sally Ride.

00:05:45.758 --> 00:05:49.512
(Singing) Remember when NASA
sent a woman to space

00:05:49.554 --> 00:05:51.389
for only six days

00:05:51.431 --> 00:05:56.978
and they gave her one hundred tampons?

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:00.773
One hundred tampons.

00:06:01.399 --> 00:06:06.487
(Laughter)

00:06:06.529 --> 00:06:08.656
And they asked, "Will that be enough?"

00:06:08.698 --> 00:06:10.909
(Music)

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:14.871
Because they didn't know
if that was enough.

00:06:14.913 --> 00:06:18.416
These are our nation's greatest minds.

00:06:18.416 --> 00:06:21.920
They are literally rocket scientists.

00:06:21.961 --> 00:06:28.426
They also tied the tampons together
by the strings like sausages.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.721
One hundred tampons.

00:06:32.138 --> 00:06:35.391
A hundred tampons.

00:06:36.643 --> 00:06:39.145
I can picture it now.

00:06:39.187 --> 00:06:40.313
Come with me.

00:06:40.939 --> 00:06:45.443
"I'm Sally Ride, and I'm going
to space for the first time.

00:06:45.443 --> 00:06:47.570
I'm walking tall.

00:06:47.612 --> 00:06:50.365
I feel so proud.

00:06:50.406 --> 00:06:54.786
Then I see a man running
panicked through the crowd.

00:06:55.703 --> 00:06:57.997
He's holding a large bag.

00:06:58.373 --> 00:07:01.459
(Laughter)

00:07:01.459 --> 00:07:04.337
I think, what can this be?

00:07:04.837 --> 00:07:09.550
And then he hands 100 tampons to me.

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:15.181
And then he hands 100 tampons to me.

00:07:15.181 --> 00:07:16.975
For one week.”

00:07:17.016 --> 00:07:19.102
(Laughter)

00:07:20.937 --> 00:07:23.731
"They could have asked me.

00:07:26.776 --> 00:07:30.405
I would have said, "Maybe 33."

00:07:30.446 --> 00:07:32.407
(Laughter)

00:07:32.448 --> 00:07:37.787
“Because even if it were my period week,

00:07:37.787 --> 00:07:41.165
I might have already
brought some with me."

00:07:41.207 --> 00:07:43.251
(Laughter)

00:07:43.292 --> 00:07:44.877
"But, you know ...

00:07:47.338 --> 00:07:49.090
Thanks, I guess."

00:07:50.049 --> 00:07:51.759
One hundred tampons.

00:07:51.801 --> 00:07:56.931
How does she use a tampon
when they tied the strings together?

00:07:58.558 --> 00:08:01.102
One hundred tampons.

00:08:01.144 --> 00:08:02.520
Don't hate me.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.816
525,600 tampons --

00:08:06.858 --> 00:08:08.026
So sorry,

00:08:08.026 --> 00:08:13.239
525,000 tampons

00:08:13.281 --> 00:08:19.454
in space.

00:08:20.246 --> 00:08:21.414
Thank you all.

00:08:21.456 --> 00:08:26.335
(Applause)

00:08:26.377 --> 00:08:28.212
Thank you so much. Thank you.

00:08:28.254 --> 00:08:30.131
(Applause)

00:08:30.131 --> 00:08:31.883
Thank you, I really appreciate it.

00:08:33.051 --> 00:08:37.430
So men on the internet
were furious about this song.

00:08:37.847 --> 00:08:39.015
Furious.

00:08:39.057 --> 00:08:41.142
And it's funny because honestly,

00:08:41.142 --> 00:08:43.770
out of all the feminist
comedy songs that I've written,

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:46.147
this is the absolute tamest by far.

00:08:46.606 --> 00:08:49.108
Like, for example, me and my
bandmate Isabel Martin,

00:08:49.108 --> 00:08:52.779
we wrote a song called “All Older White
Men Should Die (But Not My Dad).”

00:08:52.779 --> 00:08:56.032
(Laughter)

00:08:56.074 --> 00:08:59.160
So I really didn't think
this would make them that angry.

00:08:59.202 --> 00:09:00.495
I really didn't.

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:02.789
But they were enraged.

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:06.125
So I wanted to show you
some of my favorite genres

00:09:06.167 --> 00:09:08.461
of comments that I got.

00:09:08.503 --> 00:09:09.670
So this first one,

00:09:09.712 --> 00:09:13.132
we have what I call the honest critic,
just offering a critique.

00:09:13.174 --> 00:09:14.884
OK, so he starts,

00:09:14.926 --> 00:09:17.178
“It was funny at first
but then she ruined it.”

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.971
OK, all right, let's hear him out.

00:09:19.013 --> 00:09:21.182
Someone said, "Please elaborate."

00:09:21.224 --> 00:09:22.558
(Laughter)

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.811
All right, you have criticism,
let's hear it.

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:27.563
"She kept yappin' about the tampons."

00:09:27.605 --> 00:09:29.273
(Laughter)

00:09:29.315 --> 00:09:31.067
And you know what? Fair.

00:09:31.109 --> 00:09:32.902
So, you know, I'm going to take that.

00:09:32.902 --> 00:09:36.447
I mean, to be honest, the tampons
is sort of a central part of the song.

00:09:36.447 --> 00:09:37.573
(Laughter)

00:09:37.615 --> 00:09:41.994
But I'll take the note, you know,
we'll work it going forward.

00:09:42.829 --> 00:09:46.332
This next one I call
the microgravity expert.

00:09:46.332 --> 00:09:49.877
And this was an absolutely
amazing phenomenon,

00:09:49.919 --> 00:09:52.588
because what happened was

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:56.759
somehow within the three
to four minutes of my song,

00:09:56.759 --> 00:09:58.636
thousands of men on the internet

00:09:58.678 --> 00:10:02.682
became experts in microgravity
and menstruation.

00:10:03.182 --> 00:10:05.351
And I think that is so impressive,

00:10:05.351 --> 00:10:09.939
because normally that sort of takes
years to really understand.

00:10:09.981 --> 00:10:13.609
But they got it like that,
and I just think that is huge.

00:10:13.609 --> 00:10:14.777
He said, "At that time,

00:10:14.819 --> 00:10:17.405
nobody had experienced
having a period in microgravity.

00:10:17.405 --> 00:10:18.573
Relax."

00:10:18.573 --> 00:10:20.491
(Laughter)

00:10:20.491 --> 00:10:22.743
Because really, that's what
they're angry about,

00:10:22.785 --> 00:10:24.829
as they're sitting
at their computer, like,

00:10:24.871 --> 00:10:26.914
“Stop laughing! It isn’t funny!”

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:30.793
This next comment, this pissed me off

00:10:30.793 --> 00:10:32.920
more than anything.

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:35.006
This type of comment, I'll just show you.

00:10:36.257 --> 00:10:38.509
“Look up Bo Burnham, if you liked.”

00:10:38.551 --> 00:10:41.679
And then the person said,
"I did look him up and he's really good.

00:10:41.679 --> 00:10:43.264
Thanks for the suggestion."

00:10:43.306 --> 00:10:44.974
Guys, can we focus on me?

00:10:45.016 --> 00:10:46.142
(Laughter)

00:10:46.142 --> 00:10:47.894
Can we please focus on me?

00:10:48.269 --> 00:10:50.980
Bo Burnham has had three
Netflix specials, alright?

00:10:51.022 --> 00:10:53.733
I just quit my job
at customer support, OK?

00:10:53.733 --> 00:10:56.110
So let's just focus on me.

00:10:56.152 --> 00:10:57.403
OK, it's my song.

00:10:57.445 --> 00:10:58.988
OK, thank you.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:01.782
(Laughter)

00:11:01.824 --> 00:11:03.451
Thank you so much.

00:11:03.451 --> 00:11:05.119
This last one.

00:11:05.161 --> 00:11:07.038
This is what I call the essayist,

00:11:07.079 --> 00:11:10.458
and this is somebody who I’m sure
were pitching to The New York Times,

00:11:10.458 --> 00:11:13.085
and The New York Times said,
“We’re full this month.”

00:11:13.085 --> 00:11:15.213
So they decided to just
DM it to me directly,

00:11:15.213 --> 00:11:16.714
and I think that's great.

00:11:16.756 --> 00:11:19.926
This went on for several
paragraphs, I will say.

00:11:19.967 --> 00:11:22.261
"Does 100 tampons really seem
that unreasonable

00:11:22.261 --> 00:11:23.846
considering that it's NASA?"

00:11:23.888 --> 00:11:25.014
Huh?

00:11:25.389 --> 00:11:27.308
"I'm male" -- No! --

00:11:27.308 --> 00:11:29.185
(Laughter)

00:11:29.227 --> 00:11:32.480
I really thought this message
was a woman, that's ... wow!

00:11:33.606 --> 00:11:37.109
"And I understand that you wouldn't take
100 with you on a 7-day vacation,

00:11:37.151 --> 00:11:40.279
but NASA had to plan
for the absolute worst-case scenario.

00:11:40.279 --> 00:11:42.281
From a quick Google... "

00:11:42.323 --> 00:11:44.992
(Laughter)

00:11:45.034 --> 00:11:48.120
"I see most women use three
to 10 tampons per day,

00:11:48.162 --> 00:11:52.250
10 being an absolute disaster of a flow."

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:55.836
(Laughter)

00:11:55.878 --> 00:11:59.590
“An absolute disaster of a flow.”

00:11:59.632 --> 00:12:02.260
Because this is what
their fear was, wasn’t it?

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:05.137
The men at NASA, they're like,
"She's going to get up there,

00:12:05.137 --> 00:12:08.057
it's going to come out of her eyes
and her ears and her mouth.

00:12:08.057 --> 00:12:11.435
And if we don't have 100 tampons,
boys, we're in trouble.

00:12:11.435 --> 00:12:13.688
We're in real trouble up there, boys."

00:12:13.688 --> 00:12:15.273
(Laughter)

00:12:15.314 --> 00:12:17.858
So I wrote him back,
I said, "You're absolutely right.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:19.068
I'll cut the song.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.570
I'll delete it right now.
It doesn't make any sense.

00:12:21.612 --> 00:12:22.822
You're completely right."

00:12:22.822 --> 00:12:26.284
But in all honesty,
I am really, really thankful

00:12:26.325 --> 00:12:28.536
if this song started any conversation

00:12:28.577 --> 00:12:31.205
where it might otherwise
be uncomfortable about periods,

00:12:31.247 --> 00:12:32.999
that's a lot of the feedback I got.

00:12:32.999 --> 00:12:35.167
And that was the most amazing thing to me,

00:12:35.167 --> 00:12:38.170
like, people sending it in their Slack
channels to their bosses,

00:12:38.212 --> 00:12:39.922
texting it to their dads.

00:12:39.964 --> 00:12:41.757
And I just think it's really insane,

00:12:41.799 --> 00:12:44.760
as we all know that half
the population bleeds

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:48.848
and the other half has no idea
what is going on.

00:12:48.889 --> 00:12:51.309
And I'm really grateful for that.

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:52.852
And I know it sounds cheesy,

00:12:52.893 --> 00:12:54.687
but I often just find myself thinking

00:12:54.687 --> 00:12:57.315
that I really hope
Sally would have liked it.

00:12:57.356 --> 00:12:58.858
So thank you all so much.

00:12:58.899 --> 00:13:00.067
Thank you.

00:13:00.109 --> 00:13:01.902
(Applause)

